            <ArticleTitle>Huntingtin Fibrils Poke Membranes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>32-33</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0092-8674(17)31058-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cell.2017.09.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A hallmark of Huntington's disease is the presence of intracellular aggregates of mutant huntingtin, the pathological significance of which has long been debated. Using cryo-electron tomography, Bauerlein et al. reveal the fibrillary structure of huntingtin aggregates in situ and show that huntingtin fibrils interact with the endoplasmic reticulum, distorting its morphology and dynamics.</AbstractText>
